   {
    "text": "Jennifer has 10 dollars and milk costs 3 dollars per gallon. To determine how many gallons she can buy, we divide her total money by the cost per gallon. \n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{3} = 3 \\text{ gallons with a remainder of } 1 \\text{ dollar}\n$$\n\nThis means she can buy 3 full gallons of milk, and she will have 1 dollar left over. The remainder is 1 dollar, which is not enough to buy another gallon.\n\n*** \n\nThe calculation shows that the total amount of money she has is not a multiple of the cost per gallon. Since 3 gallons cost 9 dollars, she has 1 dollar remaining. This remainder is important because it indicates how much money she doesn't have enough for another gallon.\n\n*** \n\nThe remainder of 1 dollar shows that she cannot purchase a fourth gallon. This leftover money is not sufficient to buy another gallon of milk, which means she can only buy 3 gallons in total. The remainder is a key factor in determining how many full gallons she can purchase.",
